Title: gaze_close reads a real panel as seats: self-only when run from another project's cwd
Created: 2026-10-29
Author: reviewer

--- log ---
2026-10-29T20:56Z reviewer created

--- body ---
## Context
Reported by the DOIfetch session (raid f9e483, 2026-10-29) through the inter-project protocol; causes as its team lead stated them, not re-verified here.
`gaze_close.py` resolves the project from its cwd. Run from the harness directory, it read a real
panel as `seats: self-only` (DOIfetch PR #26, likely #19), which counts as no record
(`skills/merge/SKILL.md` § Gating tiers) and fired the gaze-self-only-twice breaker. This blocks merges.
Same family as the containment branch mismatch seen in harness PRs #1984 and #1996 today (the
runtime moved the gaze session's cwd during the run).

## Scope
Make gaze's close resolve the project and panel from explicit arguments or the review's own
record, never from the cwd at close time.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] A test runs the close from a foreign cwd and records the real panel's seats
- [ ] The containment check reports the review tree, not the session's moved cwd

## Imagine (raid b7c67c)
Read: gaze_close.py, gaze_setup.py, cleanup.md, SKILL.md, tests; nothing run.
Root cause, confirmed by reading (all three candidates hold, one origin):
- `main()` calls `primary_root()` with no argument: it reads `git rev-parse` in the cwd. Run from
  the harness directory for a DOIfetch review, `primary` is the harness repo. Everything keyed on it
  then misses: `panel_reading`/`seat_block`/`read_entry` build `<primary>/.claude/worktrees/review-N/
  .panel/N` (absent), `seat_clock.py seats` prints nothing, the derived line falls to
  `seats: self-only (panel unreadable)`, and `plan_check_line` reports FAIL. `cleanup` also finds no
  tree ("no review worktree") and `record_answer`/`pointer` use the wrong repo's notes and, for
  `pointer`, `forge.get_forge(None, pr)` resolves the repo from cwd too (to verify: pointer may
  comment on the wrong repo's PR N).
- The entry record (`entry-state.json`) lives inside that panel, so the record that could tell the
  close where it is is itself reachable only through the primary. Chicken and egg: a cwd-free close
  needs one explicit anchor.
- `containment()` runs `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`, `git status`, `git branch --show-current`
  with no `cwd=`, so it judges wherever the runtime left the shell. #1984/#1996/#2003: cwd moved to
  the main checkout, branch `main` != entry `t...`, exit 4 though nothing was contaminated.
  `entry-state.json` stores the entry branch and porcelain but not the entry toplevel.
Simplest fix (one anchor, no new state store):
1. `gaze_setup.py` prints `repo=<primary>` and records `session_tree=<toplevel at entry>` in
   `entry-state.json` (it already records the rest).
2. `gaze_close.py --repo <primary>` (default: cwd, so old callers behave as before; env
   `IDH_REVIEW_REPO` not needed). `primary = primary_root(cwd=a.repo)`. SKILL.md close line passes
   `--repo <repo>` from setup's output.
3. `read_entry` first; `containment()` takes `session_tree` and runs every git read with
   `cwd=session_tree` (fall back to cwd when absent: legacy entry states). The report prints the
   session tree, not cwd.
4. `pointer()` and `record_answer` run with the primary as cwd (chdir or `cwd=` into forge calls).
Alternative rejected: deriving primary from `--branch`/PR by scanning worktrees (guessy, and DOIfetch
may have several clones). Reading the setup record alone is not possible (see chicken and egg).
Premise check: ticket's causes are "as stated, not re-verified"; verified now by reading. Not
reproduced by running, so the first failing test below is the reproduction. One doubt on exit
criterion 2: "the review tree" is ambiguous. Containment exists to check the invoking session's
tree (the review tree is removed by then). I read it as the recorded entry session tree; this
loses detection of a moved cwd, which is acceptable: a moved cwd is not contamination, and the
foreign-files and branch reads still apply to the tree that matters.

## Plan (raid b7c67c)
Actions:
1. Test first (tests/test_gaze_close.py, uses gaze_fixture `make_repo`, `opened`, `one_seat`):
   a. `test_close_from_foreign_cwd_records_the_real_panel`: open review 7 in `primary`, write a
      two-item panel with a non-self report (`one_seat` or `panel(..., reports=)` so seat_clock reads
      `seats: panel`), make a second unrelated repo `other`, run CLOSE with cwd=`other` and
      `--repo primary` and a comment claiming `seats: panel`. Assert exit 0, no "self-only", the seat
      lines in the recorded answer, `plan check: PASS`, review tree removed. Fails today: exit 2
      ("panel reads seats: self-only (panel unreadable)").
   b. `test_containment_reports_the_session_tree_not_a_moved_cwd`: setup in `primary` on branch
      `feature`... (make a session worktree on a branch, run SETUP there so entry-state records it),
      then run CLOSE with cwd=`primary` on `main` and `--repo primary`; assert exit 0 and the report
      names the session tree and branch, no "differs from entry branch". Fails today: exit 4.
   c. Legacy: entry-state without `session_tree` still closes from cwd (existing tests keep passing).
2. Implement items 1-4 above in gaze_setup.py (2 lines) and gaze_close.py (`--repo` arg;
   `containment(..., tree)`; cwd for pointer).
3. Docs: SKILL.md close command gets `--repo <repo>`; cleanup.md containment paragraph says the reads
   run in the entry session tree. Both prose, same commit. Docstring of gaze_close.py usage line.
4. Gate: `pytest tests/test_gaze_close.py tests/test_review_panel_cleanup_contract.py` plus the
   gaze setup tests; run the scoped gate.
Dependencies: none upstream. Ticket 1357 (wave 2) edits `cleanup()` and `worktree_residue`; shared
with this ticket: `cleanup(primary, pr, branch)` (only via the primary it receives; I do not change
its body) and `main()` around the `cleanup(...)` call and the `containment` call just after it.
Rebase order: land 1356 first (small); 1357 then rebases over the `--repo` line in main().
Open points for the orchestrator: (a) accept the "session tree" reading of criterion 2; (b) confirm
`pointer` really resolves the repo from cwd (forge.py grep above); if so, 1356 also fixes a
wrong-repo comment, which raises its severity.

## Orchestrator decisions (raid b7c67c)
- "Review tree" in exit criterion 2 means the session tree recorded at setup: accepted.
- The executor checks whether `pointer()` and the forge calls target the right repository from a
  foreign cwd. If they comment on the wrong repository's PR, fix that in this PR with a test,
  since it is the same defect.

## Feasibility (raid b7c67c)
PASS. Read at 8ccc046f: `gaze_close.main()` calls `primary_root()` with no cwd (line ~327);
`containment()` (line 221) runs three git reads with no `cwd=`; `read_entry` (211) keys on primary;
`gaze_setup.main()` writes `entry-state.json` (line 222) and prints `review_tree=` (235), so a `repo=`
line fits. Open point (b) CONFIRMED by reading: `pointer()` calls `forge.get_forge(None, pr)` with no
cwd (line 308), and `get_forge(choice, ref, cwd=None)` returns `GhForge(None)`, so `gh` posts to the
cwd's repository: a foreign cwd comments on the wrong repo's PR N. In scope per orchestrator decision;
pass `cwd=str(primary)`. `record_answer` already passes `cwd`. `step_clock.py` reads no cwd.
Fixtures exist: `tests/gaze_fixture.py::make_repo`, `test_gaze_close.py::opened` (58), `panel` (215),
`one_seat` (224). Files shared with 1357: `scripts/gaze_close.py`, `scripts/gaze_setup.py`,
`tests/test_gaze_close.py`, `skills/gaze/SKILL.md`; wave order handles it, no coordination PR.

## Blind-spot (raid b7c67c)
- `gaze_setup.py:133` is cwd-bound too (`primary_root()`, `get_forge(None, pr)`, entry branch read in
  cwd). Setup run from the wrong project reads that repo's PR N. Out of this ticket's exit criteria, but
  the SKILL.md setup line should say "run from the reviewed project's checkout"; `--repo` on setup is
  the symmetric fix if cheap (same one-arg pattern). Executor decides; name it in the PR body if deferred.
- Naming: verify-gate already takes `repo=<path>` (`skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md:21-25`). Setup's
  printed `repo=` and close's `--repo` match it; keep that spelling.
- Runtimes: Codex and Pi read the same `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` (no gaze-specific adapter; only
  `adapters/README.md` mentions gaze), so the doc change reaches them; the default-to-cwd keeps old
  callers working.

